# =============================================================================
# Guardrail Service — Production Environment Reference
# =============================================================================
# Reference template — fill in for your deployment. Not loaded by the app
# itself; production is configured by whichever mechanism your deployment
# uses (K8s ConfigMap/Secret, systemd EnvironmentFile=) to populate the host
# environment.
#
# GUARDRAIL OWNS POLICY, NOT ENGINES.
#
# There is deliberately NO provider selector and NO engine sub-config here.
# This service hosts no LLM and holds no vendor credential: judgement is
# delegated to `apps/text` (which owns the provider adapters, the BYOK plane
# and the circuit breakers) and classification/NER to `apps/nlp`. The
# provider/model pair is DB-resolved per tenant and FAILS CLOSED when
# unresolved — there is no env engine to fall back to.
#
# The six engine prefixes `GUARDRAIL_{OLLAMA,OPENAI_COMPAT,AZURE,BEDROCK,VLLM,
# LLAMA_CPP}_*` and `GUARDRAIL_GLINER_*` were DELETED (TASK-735/736). Setting
# one has no effect. A previous version of this file documented that deleted
# engine plane and was removed for it (finding F-03) — this one exists because
# `env:sync --check` now makes it falsifiable: a key here with no reader fails
# the gate.
#
# HISTORY — read before deleting this file again.
# TASK-799 lane B DELETED apps/guardrail/.env.prod on 2026-08-23 (a40158536, F-03),
# and it was right to: "every line of it was the deleted vLLM/llama.cpp plane, told
# operators to set a GUARDRAIL_V2_PROVIDER that has no field and no reader, and
# hardcoded granite-guardian-4.1-8b."
#
# This file is NOT that file restored. It documents the bootstrap floor that really
# exists and explicitly disclaims the plane that does not. The objective test F-03
# would have wanted is the one that now guards it: `pnpm env:sync --check` fails on
# any key here with no reader (READER_CHECKED_FILES in scripts/env-sync.mts). If a
# future audit finds this file lying again, that gate broke first — fix the gate,
# then the file.
#
# NOTE ON PREFIXES — this service carries TWO, and both are live. The root
# settings class is `GUARDRAIL_V2_`; the surviving per-concern sub-configs are
# `GUARDRAIL_` (database) and `GUARDRAIL_REDIS_`. Check which class you are
# configuring before naming a variable.
# =============================================================================

# --- The one shared internal service credential (owner decision D-D) --------
# Identical across every HOPE service. What this service ACCEPTS as inbound
# `X-Service-Token` and what it PRESENTS on every outbound peer call.
INTERNAL_ACCESS_TOKEN=

# --- Tenant config plane (the sanctioned direct-SQL exception) ---------------
# Guardrail's callers are PEER services (apps/text posts to it directly,
# forwarding only `X-Tenant-Id`), so there is no gateway to inject config, and
# pulling it over HTTP would put the gateway on the safety-critical path and
# close a gateway -> text -> guardrail -> gateway cycle. Hence a READ-ONLY
# direct read. The exception covers the TRANSPORT and nothing else: resolution
# is still tenant -> SYSTEM, two tiers, fail-closed.
GUARDRAIL_DATABASE_URL=postgresql+asyncpg://guardrail_reader:CHANGE_ME@hope-postgres:5432/hope?ssl=require

# --- Peer addresses -----------------------------------------------------------
# `TEXT_URL` and `NLP_URL` are REPO-WIDE bootstrap transport, read here via
# AliasChoices so guardrail adds no env var of its own. Set them once for the
# deployment; they are declared in the platform config, not per-service.
